skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- enact ---
task: We just added a lint step to this repo; it runs with `just lint`. Write that down somewhere it'll stick, so whoever picks this up next runs it. Make the file edits now, in this repo.
--- setup ---
git init -q
mkdir -p specs
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# todo-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Build: `cargo build`
- Test: `cargo test`

## Style
Run `cargo fmt` before committing.
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: todo-cli

## Mission
A small command-line to-do manager.

## Architecture
One Rust binary.

## Tech Stack
Rust.

## Layout
src/main.rs.

## Testing
Unit tests are load-bearing; commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
cargo install.
MD
git add -A && git commit -q -m "seed" || true
--- assert ---
set -e
# The operational command landed in AGENTS.md (the operational layer).
grep -qF 'just lint' AGENTS.md
# project.md did not become the operational home for it. The rule is that
# AGENTS.md owns the command and project.md Testing keeps "only the layer
# semantics" — which permits a pointer like "commands live in AGENTS.md and
# include `just lint`". A bare substring ban cannot tell a pointer from a
# duplicate, and would fail this very repo's project.md, which names
# `just lint` and `just test` six times. So check the thing that matters:
# project.md must not carry a runnable command line or a commands section of
# its own.
test -z "$(grep -iE '^#+.*(commands|how to run|build & test)' specs/project.md || true)"
# No fenced command block was added to project.md (a prose mention like
# "verified by `just lint`" is layer semantics; a copy-pasteable block is
# the operational content that belongs in AGENTS.md).
test -z "$(awk '/^```/{f=!f;next} f && /^[[:space:]]*(just|cargo) /{print}' specs/project.md || true)"
# AGENTS.md stays a lean, convention-clean file — no kdevkit scaffold
# leaked in (no six-section headers, no HTML-comment prompts, no logs).
test -z "$(grep -iE 'Session Log|Decision Log|Active initiatives|Feature Brief|Implementation Plan' AGENTS.md || true)"
test -z "$(grep -F '<!--' AGENTS.md || true)"
